{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"permier-bold-goat","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# An on-demand CI job that MAPS the Jira DC image, so we can declare its contract instead of discovering it\n\n## Why this is the right instrument\n\nThe DC image is **amd64-only and exists only inside CI** — the development host is arm64, which is\nwhy the previous AC verification pass recorded \"no live cell was run during verification\". Every\nquestion about that image is therefore currently answered the same way: ship a change, wait ~35\nminutes, read the failure. Today that loop was spent three times on ONE question (does the project\nhave an `Epic` type?), and the answer arrived as a SETUP FAILED at minute 35.\n\nAn on-demand GitHub Actions job that boots the pinned image and interrogates it collapses that loop\nto a single run, and its OUTPUT is the environment contract that [rebar:3fe5-c22e-dc2d-4c9e] needs\nwritten down.\n\n## The load-bearing constraint: this is an AUTHORING tool, not a test-path component\n\n**The deliverable is committed DATA, not a running agent.** If the agent ever executes on the normal\nharness path, we have reintroduced exactly the runtime discovery the operator just ruled against —\nwith more non-determinism and an LLM in the loop. It must be:\n\n * **manually dispatched** (`workflow_dispatch`), never on push/PR/schedule;\n * **producing a committed artifact** — the declared capability contract plus the raw request/response\n   evidence behind it — which a human reviews and lands;\n * **run again only when the image is re-pinned**, as the way we re-derive the contract for a new\n   digest.\n\nIts relationship to the harness is the same as a spike's to a story: it answers questions once so the\nanswers can be written down.\n\n## What it should answer — a CHECKLIST, not open-ended exploration\n\nAn unscoped \"go explore\" agent returns prose. This should answer specific questions and emit\nstructured results:\n\n**Inventory (what is there by default)**\n * exact image digest, Jira version, and licensed applications (Software present? — that determines\n   whether `Epic` exists at all)\n * project templates the instance actually offers (`/rest/project-templates/latest/templates`)\n * for a project created from EACH candidate template: the issue types it yields\n * the full field inventory (`/rest/api/2/field`) — specifically the ids of `Epic Link` and\n   `Epic Name`, which differ per instance\n * default screen configuration: is `Epic Link` on a standard issue's EDIT screen? is `Epic Name` on\n   the Epic CREATE screen?\n\n**Experiments (validate the configuration hooks BEFORE we depend on them)**\n * can the admin PAT create an issue type via `POST /rest/api/2/issuetype` and add it to a project's\n   scheme? — the deterministic alternative to template roulette\n * can it add a field to a screen? Confirm by reading `editmeta` back\n * **the two falsifiers recorded on [rebar:37e7-d751-0042-4b94]**: what operations does `parent`\n   expose in a sub-task's `editmeta`, and does `{\"update\":{\"parent\":[{\"set\":null}]}}` behave\n   differently from `{\"fields\":{\"parent\":null}}`? A difference overturns that ticket's\n   NOT-RESOLVABLE verdict\n * **[rebar:1a9f-50c0-e7a5-4fda] AC1**: does a sub-task `PUT fields.parent` return 2xx-and-ignore, or\n   4xx? Raw REST, no rebar in the path\n * once an Epic exists: does writing `Epic Link` on a child actually take effect — the question\n   change 1311 has now failed to reach three times\n\n**Every answer must carry its raw request and response.** The contract must be traceable to evidence,\nnot to an agent's summary — otherwise we have swapped \"discovering from the instance\" for \"trusting a\nmodel\", which is worse.\n\n## Caveats worth designing against\n\n * **Pin by digest in this job too**, or the map describes an image we do not run.\n * **Boot time is minutes**; batch every question into one run rather than iterating.\n * The job holds an admin PAT on a throwaway instance — acceptable, but it must touch nothing\n   outside it, and must not be reachable from a fork PR.\n * The agent must be told to REPORT rather than FIX. A mapping job that starts mutating the harness\n   is how a spike turns into an unreviewed change.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a `workflow_dispatch`-only workflow boots the digest-pinned DC image and runs the checklist\n      above, emitting a structured artifact (raw request/response per question)\n- [ ] the artifact answers, unambiguously: which template yields `Epic`; the `Epic Link` /\n      `Epic Name` field ids; whether those fields are on the needed screens by default; and whether\n      creating an issue type + editing a screen via REST works with the harness's credentials\n- [ ] the 37e7 and 1a9f falsifiers are executed and their raw responses recorded on those tickets\n- [ ] the resulting environment contract is COMMITTED as data and consumed by the harness's setup\n      assertion — it does NOT re-derive anything at test time\n- [ ] the workflow cannot run on push, PR, or schedule, and this is asserted, not merely configured\n- [ ] a short doc states when to re-run it (image re-pin) and that its output is authored, not live","id":"259b-b7da-a346-4785","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"On-demand CI job that maps the Jira DC image, so its contract can be declared not discovered"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785798175514064001,"uuid":"d60c39e3-155b-4bba-aa3b-8c76691dcba3"}